.TH LS.LUA 1 "November 2026" "ls.lua 0.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ls.lua \- list directory contents, written in Lua
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ls.lua
[\fB\-a1l\fR]
[\fIDIR\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ls.lua
is a small
.BR ls (1)
clone written in Lua 5.4 with
.BR luaposix .
It lists the entries of each
.I DIR
argument.
When no
.I DIR
is given, the current directory
.RB ( . )
is listed.
.PP
Entries are sorted by Lua string byte order (locale-independent).
Names that begin with a dot
.RB ( . )
are omitted unless
.B \-a
is given.
.PP
When standard output is a terminal and
.B \-l
is not used, names are printed on a single line separated by two spaces,
with ANSI colours applied (see
.BR COLOURS ).
When standard output is not a terminal, or when
.B \-1
is given, one name is printed per line.
.B \-l
always prints one entry per line in long format, regardless of whether
the output is a terminal.
.PP
When more than one
.I DIR
is given, each directory is introduced with a header line of the form
.IB DIR :
and a blank line is inserted before every header after the first.
.PP
Options may be clustered after a single leading dash
(for example
.BR \-al ).
There are no long options, and a bare
.B \-
is treated as a path, not as an option.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-a
Do not hide entries whose names begin with a dot.
This includes
.B .
and
.B ..
when they are present in the directory listing.
.TP
.B \-l
Use a long listing format.
Each line shows, in order: the file mode string, the hard-link count,
the owner name (or numeric UID if the name cannot be resolved), the group
name (or numeric GID), the size in bytes, the modification time, and the
name.
.IP
The mode string begins with
.B d
for a directory,
.B l
for a symbolic link, or
.B \-
otherwise, followed by the nine
.BR rwx
permission bits for user, group and other.
The modification time is formatted as
.B %b %e %H:%M
(month, day-of-month, hour and minute) in the local timezone; the year is
never shown.
.IP
Symbolic links are not followed for type or metadata
.RB ( lstat (2)
semantics).
For a symlink the name is followed by
.B \->
and the link target; if the target cannot be read, a question mark
.RB ( ? )
is shown instead.
.TP
.B \-1
List one entry per line.
This is the default when standard output is not a terminal.
Ignored in effect when
.B \-l
is also given, because long format already prints one entry per line.
.SH COLOURS
Colouring is applied only when standard output is a terminal
.RB ( isatty (3)
on file descriptor 1) and a
.BR lstat (2)
result is available for the entry.
Hard-coded ANSI SGR sequences are used; there is no
.B LS_COLORS
or other colour configuration.
.PP
.TP
directory
bold blue
.RB ( \\e033[1;34m )
.TP
symbolic link
bold cyan
.RB ( \\e033[1;36m )
.TP
executable
bold green
.RB ( \\e033[1;32m )
.TP
other
no colour
.PP
An entry is treated as executable when any of the user, group or other
execute bits is set (mode mask octal 0111).
Colour escapes are reset with
.B \\e033[0m
after each coloured name.
.PP
When output is not a terminal, names are printed without escape sequences.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B ls.lua
does not read any environment variables.
In particular,
.BR LS_COLORS ,
.BR COLUMNS ,
.B TZ
and locale variables are ignored for listing, colouring and time display
(the modification time uses Lua's
.B os.date
with the process local timezone).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every requested directory was listed successfully.
.TP
.B 2
An unknown option character was given, or at least one directory could not
be opened.
On an invalid option,
.B ls.lua
writes a message of the form
.IP
.B ls.lua: invalid option -- '\fIc\fB'
.IP
to standard error and exits immediately without listing anything.
On a directory open failure it writes a diagnostic to standard error,
continues with any remaining directories, and exits 2 at the end.
.SH EXAMPLES
List the current directory with colours (on a terminal):
.IP
.B ls.lua
.PP
Include hidden entries:
.IP
.B ls.lua \-a
.PP
Long listing of
.IR /etc :
.IP
.B ls.lua \-l /etc
.PP
Combine options; one name per line including hidden files:
.IP
.B ls.lua \-a1
.PP
List several directories with headers:
.IP
.B ls.lua /tmp /var/tmp
.PP
Force plain output suitable for scripts (no colours, one per line) by
redirecting away from a terminal:
.IP
.B ls.lua > names.txt
.SH NOTES
.B ls.lua
is intentionally tiny.
It does not implement recursive listing
.RB ( \-R ),
human-readable sizes
.RB ( \-h ),
inode numbers
.RB ( \-i ),
almost-all
.RB ( \-A ),
directory-only
.RB ( \-d ),
sorting by time or size, columnised multi-column layout that respects
terminal width,
.BR \-\-color ,
or POSIX
.B \-\-
end-of-options.
Any argument that begins with a dash and has at least one following
character is parsed as a cluster of short options, so
.B \-\-help
and similar long-option spellings are rejected as an invalid option
character
.RB ( \- )
rather than being ignored or treated as paths.
.PP
Permission bits for setuid, setgid and sticky are not rendered specially
in the mode string (the nine
.BR rwx
slots are shown as
.B x
or
.B \-
only).
File types other than directory and symlink (FIFO, socket, block/char
device) are shown with a leading
.B \-
rather than the usual
.BR p / s / b / c
letters.
.PP
Owner and group lookups use
.BR getpwuid (3)
and
.BR getgrgid (3);
if a name cannot be resolved the numeric id is printed instead.
.SH BUGS
Multi-column terminal output is a single space-separated line, not a
width-aware grid like GNU
.BR ls .
Very wide directories therefore wrap poorly.
.PP
The long-format time never shows a year, so entries older than about six
months are still printed with month and day only.
.PP
A failing
.BR lstat (2)
on an individual entry is not reported; the corresponding row may then
lack colour or long-format fields may error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ls (1),
.BR readdir (3),
.BR stat (2),
.BR lstat (2),
.BR readlink (2),
.BR isatty (3)
.PP
